{"author":{"id":"127a58c7f014820991be95805df42525db12c92e2d7319d04d4df105a9aa3444"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1788350813,"nonce":"EEjX7aDWt0m7pjvvskQun7FAn8U=","title":"Run `m1-20260901`: the freezer was placed as a layout, built, and **never enclosed**. `room-at` on its interior returned `outdoors: true, cells: 60082` — the great outdoors — for the rest of the run. The colony therefore had no larder, ran hand-to-mouth into winter, and starved. This is one of the three defects named as the cause of the wipe.","message":"The agent could not see it. `room-at` answers for one cell, honestly, and nothing connects a *placed layout that was supposed to be a room* to the question *is it a room yet*. Finding this out costs a cell-by-cell interrogation the agent has no reason to run on a layout that reported `placed` with zero blockers.\n\n\n1. `construction {layout_id:\"ly-N\"}` reports enclosure for any layout whose IR declares an enclosed space: whether the cells it was meant to enclose form a proper room, or are still part of the outdoors.\n2. Where it is not enclosed, the report **names the gap** — the unbuilt or missing wall/door cells that leave it open — so the agent can act rather than merely know. A boolean `enclosed: false` repeats the defect one level up.\n3. `rooms` lists intended-but-unenclosed layouts, so the failure is reachable from a turn-level read and not only from a caller that already suspects a specific layout.\n4. A layout that is still unenclosed across a day boundary is surfaced the way `B-1`'s stalled blueprints are; the two roll-ups should read consistently, since an unenclosed room and a stalled blueprint are usually the same event seen from two sides.\n5. `checklists/turn.md` and `templates/freezer-kitchen.md` cite it. The template is the one that shipped this failure.\n6. `accept/\u003cid\u003e-enclosure.py` on a bench: place a room layout, leave one wall cell unbuilt, advance a day, assert the agent is told it is not enclosed and told which cell is open.\n\n\nC1 of `next-run.md`: *\"I never looked at the base after day 1.\"* One `render` on the first morning would have shown a freezer with a hole in it. The code fix is the deliverable here; the playbook lesson that a numbers-only read cannot see a hole in a wall ships in the same commit.\n\n\nThis is item 2 of the repair round's bench list: *place a room layout, leave one wall cell unbuilt, advance a day, the agent is told the room is not enclosed without asking cell-by-cell.*","files":null}]}